    "golden-4-wiki-lead": {
      "text": "The parish church of St Aldhelm stands on a low rise at the western edge of the village, overlooking the water meadows of the River Stour. The present building dates largely from the fourteenth century, although fragments of Norman masonry survive in the north wall of the nave. The tower, completed around 1420, holds a ring of six bells, the oldest cast in 1598 by a Salisbury founder. Restoration work in 1868 under the direction of a local architect removed the box pews and replaced the chancel roof. The church was designated a Grade I listed building in 1961 and remains in regular use for worship.",
